  "query_preview": "..\nSynopsis:\n\nThere is an upper trough overhead that is bringing precipitation\nin the form of rain and snow currently. There has been up to a\nquarter inch of precipitation accumulation in a few locations so\nfar, and this activity will end after 6 PM. Snow up to one inch of\naccumulation is expected in elevations above 2700 feet, with\nisolated locations seeing a bit more than this, maybe 3 inches at\nthe maximum on grassy surfaces. Some of this snow will likely melt\nthough.\n\nLow clouds will slowly fade in the south for tonight and then\nreturn again tomorrow morning as the trough maintains its\nposition. There is a chance for light rain and snow north of HWY 2\nbetween midnight and 6 am.\n\nThe overall trough pattern will eventually become a split between\na trough and ridge on Thursday, so temperatures are expected to\nstay in the 40s and 50s for highs after that. The next chance for\nprecipitation appears to be on Friday night, but there is limited\nconfidence on precipitation amounts on the weekend.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThere are no deviations from the base forecast.\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence of precipitation ending after\n6 PM tonight, and then occurring again briefly between midnight \nand 6 am north of HWY 2.\n\nThere is high confidence of high temperatures remaining in the 40s\nand 50s through the end of the week.",
